 "reference_provenance": "recomputed",
 "provenance_note": "Every reference value was re-derived at run time from its cited primary source by /root/bench3/ref/gen_heat2d.py (Fourier / bipolar / erf / plane-wall / Bessel series, an MFS solve of the periodic buried-pipe row, and an independently written second-order finite-volume scheme that supplies the discretization tolerances). No value was read back out of benchmarks/heat2d.json.",
 "reference": "Own python implementation of closed-form solutions of the 2-D heat equation: the Fourier-series rectangular plate (Incropera eq. 4.19), the cylindrical-shell log law (eq. 3.28), a bipolar limiting-point solution for eccentric cylinders cross-checked against the arccosh form of Table 4.1 case 7, a method-of-fundamental-solutions evaluation of the buried-pipe row shape factor cross-checked against the closed-form line-source row factor and the exact single-pipe factor (Table 4.1 case 1), the erf semi-infinite solid, and the plane-wall and infinite-cylinder eigenfunction series (full 200-term series, not truncated to one term, cross-checked against the one-term approximation). Eigenvalues from scipy.brentq on analytically guaranteed brackets. The tolerance for the mesh-dependent cases is taken from the discretization error of an independently written second-order finite-volume scheme on the same mesh, not from what makes the tool pass.",
 "reference_citations": [
  "F.P. Incropera, D.P. DeWitt, T.L. Bergman, A.S. Lavine, \"Fundamentals of Heat and Mass Transfer\", 6th ed., Wiley 2007: eq. 3.28, eq. 4.19, Table 4.1 cases 1 and 7, eq. 5.39a/5.43/5.47a/5.52, eq. 5.60, Table 5.1.",
  "H.S. Carslaw, J.C. Jaeger, \"Conduction of Heat in Solids\", 2nd ed., Oxford University Press 1959: Sec. 2.5 (semi-infinite solid), Sec. 3.4 (slab with surface convection), Sec. 7.6 (cylinder with surface convection), Sec. 4.4 (buried line sources).",
  "M.M. Yovanovich, \"Conduction and Thermal Contact Resistances (Conductances)\", in W.M. Rohsenow, J.P. Hartnett, Y.I. Cho (eds.), Handbook of Heat Transfer, 3rd ed., McGraw-Hill 1998, Ch. 3 (conduction shape factors)."
 ],
 "tolerance": "Per case, stated in each case entry. Steady Cartesian cases where the exact solution is linear are held to 0.002 K (the discrete scheme is exact there); mesh-dependent Cartesian cases to 2.5-3x the discretization error of an independent second-order FV scheme on the same mesh; curved (staircase) boundaries to a half-cell displacement of the Dirichlet surface propagated through the exact solution, plus a Richardson extrapolation in dx^1 to 0.5-1 %; transient cases to 0.25-1 % of the temperature step, which is the tool's own declared per-step time tolerance carried over the run. Never chosen from what makes the tool pass.",
 "not_benchmarked": {
  "sphere": "The tool solves the two-dimensional Cartesian heat equation on a square grid.  It has no axisymmetric or spherical mode, so the Heisler sphere solution cannot be set up in it at all and is not benchmarked.  The infinite cylinder, by contrast, IS a plane cross-section and is benchmarked above.",
  "3d_shape_factors": "The edge factor S = 0.54 D and the corner factor S = 0.15 L (Incropera Table 4.1 cases 8 and 9) are three-dimensional constructs.  A plane 2-D model has no such degree of freedom, so they are not benchmarked.  The 2-D shape factors that ARE expressible (buried pipe / row, eccentric cylinders, cylindrical shell) are benchmarked above."
 },
